# Project identity

This repo is a **framework and starter** built on [Better Auth](https://better-auth.com) for shipping your **own authentication / identity server**.

## Vision

Build toward a more **open, federated, and decentralized** identity future: standards-based issuers anyone can run, apps that federate via OAuth / OIDC, and accounts that can link protocol and wallet identities — not closed login silos.

## What it is

- An **authorization server / OpenID Connect provider** you own and deploy.
- Meant to be used by other apps as a shared identity host: users sign in here; client apps get tokens via standard OAuth 2.1 / OIDC flows.
- Not “just add login to one Next.js app.” The Next.js app *is* the auth server (UI + API + discovery + admin).
- Bridges **web2 auth** (email, passkeys, OIDC) with **decentralized / crypto identity** so one issuer can cover both.
- **Billing** is planned (subscriptions / payments tied to the same account model — not shipped yet).

**Public copy:** do **not** name third-party IdP brands or hostnames in docs, README, marketing, or Linear. Describe the pattern generically (“own IdP”, “accounts-style”, “shared identity host”, “open / federated / decentralized”).

## Two ways to use it

Consumers choose one of these shapes; both are intentional:

| Mode | What you do | Like |
|---|---|---|
| **Extend the template** | Keep this Next.js app, build product UI here, run the full frontend + auth server together | Monolith starter you grow into a product |
| **Accounts-style IdP** | Deploy this primarily as the auth/accounts host; other apps are separate OAuth/OIDC clients | Shared issuer + many client apps |

Do not assume one mode. Prefer designs that work in both: solid issuer/API/consent/admin first; first-party marketing/app pages are optional surface on top. Eject/follow workflow for product forks: [`.agents/skills/eject-and-follow/SKILL.md`](../../.agents/skills/eject-and-follow/SKILL.md) (index: [`AGENTS.md`](../../AGENTS.md)).

## Identity & protocol capabilities

Classic IdP:

- Email/password, passkeys, 2FA, invites, optional orgs
- OAuth 2.1 / OIDC provider (`@better-auth/oauth-provider`), discovery, consent, admin clients
- **Billing** (planned) — account-scoped plans/payments that fit either usage mode

Decentralized / social / wallet (product surface — implement or extend toward these):

| Capability | Notes |
|---|---|
| **Nostr** | Sign-in + key linking (`better-auth-nostr`) — currently wired |
| **Bluesky** | AT Proto identity / login linking |
| **Bitcoin Connect** | BTC wallet connect as an auth/link method |
| **Lightning** | Lightning Network auth / payments-adjacent identity |
| **ETH** | Ethereum wallet sign-in / account linking (e.g. SIWE-style) |

Treat these as first-class **sign-in and account-link methods** on the IdP, not as separate products. Protocol-specific OAuth scopes (Nostr, AT Proto, wallet, etc.) may be reserved for resources — see `docs/client-trust-model.md`.

## Mental model

| Role | Analogy |
|---|---|
| This deploy | Your IdP / accounts host (optionally plus your product UI) |
| Other products / SPAs / APIs | OAuth / OIDC clients (same deploy in extend mode, or separate hosts in IdP mode) |
| End users | Accounts living on *your* issuer (email, Nostr, Bluesky, BTC/LN, ETH, …) |

Discovery and protocol surfaces (see `docs/features.md`):

- `/.well-known/openid-configuration`
- `/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server`
- Authorize / token / userinfo under `/api/auth/oauth2/*`
- Consent UI, admin client management

Implementation uses `@better-auth/oauth-provider` (successor to the older `oidcProvider` plugin), not a third-party IdP as the source of truth.

## How agents should treat work

- Prefer changes that strengthen **IdP / AS** behavior: clients, scopes, consent, trust tiers, discovery, issuer/JWT, admin ops, deployability.
- Prefer identity methods that plug into the same account model (link + sign-in), especially Nostr, Bluesky, Bitcoin Connect, Lightning, and ETH.
- Prefer openness: federated OIDC clients, DCR with earned privileges, portable / decentralized identity links.
- Design for **both** extend-the-template and accounts-style separation; avoid coupling that forces one mode.
- Treat billing as planned account-layer work, not a separate product silo.
- Keep first-party app auth UX (sign-in, account) in service of the server; product frontend is optional in IdP-only setups.
- When explaining or documenting, lead with “own open / federated / decentralized identity server,” not “Next.js auth boilerplate.”
